.TH CChoiceDialog n 03/08/98 "CADC"
.\".BS
.SH NAME
CChoiceDialog 
\- Create and manipulate a widget which displays a dialog with choices (radio buttons).

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBCChoiceDialog\fI \fIpathName \fR?\fIoptions\fR?

.SH "INHERITANCE"
itk::Toplevel \<- util::TopLevelWidget \<- util::DialogWidget \<- CChoiceDialog

.SO
\-background	\-cursor	\-font
\-foreground	\-highlightcolor	\-highlightthickness

.SE

.SH "INHERITED OPTIONS"
.LP
.nf
.ta 4c 8c 12c
\fB\-center\fR	\fB\-clientdata\fR	\fB\-number\fR
\fB\-shorthelpwin\fR	\fB\-standalone\fR	\fB\-title\fR
\fB\-transient\fR	\fB\-withdraw\fR
.LP
See the \fBTopLevelWidget\fR class manual entry for details on the 
inherited options.

.LP
.nf
.ta 4c 8c 12c
\fB\-bitmap\fR	\fB\-buttons\fR	\fB\-default\fR
\fB\-image\fR	\fB\-justify\fR	\fB\-messagefont\fR
\fB\-messagewidth\fR	\fB\-modal\fR	\fB\-text\fR
.LP
See the \fBDialogWidget\fR class manual entry for details on the 
inherited options.


.SH "WIDGET-SPECIFIC OPTIONS"

.OP \-choice choice Choice
Specifies the choices that are to appear on the dialog.  Any 
Tcl/Tk list is valid.  Each element in the list must be unique
or errors will occur.


.OP \-cols cols Columns
Specifies the maximum number of columns the choices are to be
displayed in.  Note only one of \-cols and \-rows can be specified.
A value of 0, means infinite.
Default value is 0.

.OP \-command command Command
Specifies a command which is executed whenever the choice is
altered.

.OP \-orient orient Orient
Specifies the orientation of the choice list with respect to the 
image and text. The only valid values are
"horizontal" and \"vertical\". If horizontal is given then the 
choices appear to the right of the text and image, if vertical is given 
then the choices appears below the text and image.
Default value is horizontal.

.OP \-rows rows Rows
Specifies the maximum number of rows the choices are to be
displayed in.  Note only one of \-cols and \-rows can be specified.
A value of 0, means infinite.
Default value is 0.


.OP \-value value Value
Specifies the choice to be selected.  Any value given in the list
specified by the \-choice option is valid.

.OP \-variable variable Variable
Specifies the variable which is to contain the selected choice.


.\".BE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBCChoiceDialog\fR command creates a new window (given by the
\fIpathName\fR argument) and makes it into a \fBCChoiceDialog\fR widget.
Additional options, described above, may be specified on the 
command line or in the option database to configure aspects of
the choice dialog. The \fBCChoiceDialog\fR command returns its
\fIpathName\fR argument.  At the time this command is invoked,
there must not exist a window named \fIpathName\fR, but
\fIpathName\fR's parent must exist.

.SH "INHERITED COMMANDS"
.LP
.nf
.ta 4c 8c 12c
\fBadd_help_button\fR	\fBadd_menu_short_help\fR	\fBadd_menubar\fR
\fBadd_menubutton\fR	\fBadd_menuitem\fR	\fBadd_short_help\fR
\fBbusy\fR	\fBcenter_window\fR	\fBconfigure_menubutton\fR
\fBget_menu\fR	\fBget_menubutton\fR	\fBhide_windows\fR
\fBlist_windows\fR	\fBmake_short_help\fR	\fBquit\fR
\fBshow_help\fR	\fBtest\fR
.fi 
.LP
See the \fButil::TopLevelWidget\fR class manual entry for details on the 
inherited commands

.SH "WIDGET COMMAND"
The \fBCChoiceDialog\fR command creates a new Tcl command whose
name is \fIpathName\fR.  This
command may be used to invoke various
operations on the widget.  It has the following general form:
.CS
\fIpathName method \fR?\fIarg arg ...\fR?
.CE
\fImethod\fR and the \fIarg\fRs
determine the exact behavior of the command.  
.PP
The following methods are possible for CChoiceDialog widgets:

.TP
\fIpathName \fBactivate\fR
Display the dialogue and wait based on the modality.
Wait until the "OK" button has been selected, waits for
the result.  The result will be returned and the dialogue
is destroyed.


.TP
\fIpathName \fBclear\fR
Clears any selections made. Deselects any radio buttons that
have been selected.

.TP
\fIpathName \fBcget\fR \fIoption\fR
Returns the current value of the configuration option given
by \fIoption\fR.
\fIOption\fR may have any of the values accepted by the \fBCChoiceDialog\fR
command.

.TP
\fIpathName \fBchildsite\fR
Return the path name of the child site.

.TP
\fIpathName \fBcomponent\fR ?\fIname\fR? ?\fIcommand arg arg ...\fR?
Used to query or access component widgets within the choice dialogue
widget.  With no arguments, this returns a list of
symbolic names for component widgets that are accessible in the
current scope.  
.sp
If a symbolic \fIname\fR is specified this method returns the window
path name for that component.
.sp
Otherwise, the \fIcommand\fR and any remaining \fIarg\fR arguments
are invoked as a method on the component with the symbolic \fIname\fR.
This provides a well-defined mechanism for accessing internal components
without relying on specific window path names.

.TP
\fIpathName \fBconfig\fR
See \fBconfigure\fI.

.TP
\fIpathName\fR \fBconfigure\fR ?\fIoption\fR? ?\fIvalue option value ...\fR?
Query or modify the configuration options of the widget.
If no \fIoption\fR is specified, returns a list describing all of
the available options for \fIpathName\fR (see \fBTk_ConfigureInfo\fR for
information on the format of this list).  If \fIoption\fR is specified
with no \fIvalue\fR, then the command returns a list describing the
one named option (this list will be identical to the corresponding
sublist of the value returned if no \fIoption\fR is specified).  If
one or more \fIoption\-value\fR pairs are specified, then the command
modifies the given widget option(s) to have the given value(s);  in
this case the command returns an empty string.
\fIOption\fR may have any of the values accepted by the \fBCChoiceDialog\fR
command.

.TP
\fIpathName \fBdeactivate\fR
Removes the window from the X display and returns the current
selection.

.TP 
\fIpathName \fBgetChoice\fR
Returns the current choice that is selected.

.TP
\fIpathName \fBisa\fR \fBclassName\fR
Sets the choice dialogue's class to the specified class name.

.TP
\fIpathName \fBsetChoice\fR \fIselection\fR
Sets the current choice to the given selection.  The \fIselection\fR
must be one of the list entries in the list specified by the
\-choice option.

.SH "COMPONENTS"
.LP
.nf
Name:   \fBbot\fR
Class:  \fBFrame\fR
.fi
.IP
The bottom part of the dialogue.  This part of the dialogue
contains any buttons that are specified.  See the
\fBframe\fR manual entry for more information.

.LP
.nf
Name:   \fBbutton\fIi\fR
Class:  \fBButton\fR
.fi
.IP
This is the \fIi\fRth button at the bottom of the dialogue.  See the
\fBbutton\fR manual entry for more information.

.LP
.nf
Name:   \fBchoice\fR
Class:  \fBFrame\fR
.fi
.IP
This frame is a place holder for the choice display area.  It will 
contain the choices specified by the \-choice option.  See the
\fBframe\fR manual entry for more information.

.LP
.nf
Name:   \fBchoice\fIi\fR
Class:  \fBRadiobutton\fR
.fi
.IP
This is the \fIi\fRth radio button (choice) that is displayed in
the choice area.  Each one of these should correspond to an element
in the list specified by the \-choice option. See the
\fBradiobutton\fR manual entry for more information.

.LP
.nf
Name:   \fBdefault\fR
Class:  \fBFrame\fR
.fi
.IP
The component is the ring which appears around the default button.  
See the \fBframe\fR manual entry for more information.

.SH "DEFAULT BINDINGS"
.PP
No bindings are defined for this widget except those defined for the
widgets that are inherited.
.PP
See the ESO \fBTopLevelWidget\fR and \fBDialogWidget\fB manual entries
for more information.


.SH EXAMPLE
.DS
 set myVar ""
 CChoiceDialog .c -choice {"choice 1" "choice 2" "choice 3"}		\\
	-text "Please select one." -modal 1
 .c configure -orient vertical -title test -cols 2
 .c configure -value "choice 2" -variable "myVar"
 .c activate
 puts "Selection is: $myVar"
.DE

.SH AUTHOR
Shannon Jaeger

.SH KEYWORDS
dialog, choice, TopLevelWidget, DialogWidget, ESO
